X-SOURCE-ID:libcal_bccls_10286
X-SOURCE:Libcal Bccls 10286
DTSTART:20261210T190000Z
DTEND:20261210T200000Z
DTSTAMP:20261210T000000Z
SUMMARY:Comic Strips and Kimchi: Cooking Korean Food Made Easy with Robin Ha
DESCRIPTION:Cooking authentic Korean food at home doesn't have to be 
 intimidating or complicated—in fact\, it can be as fun and engaging as 
 reading your favorite graphic novel! Join us for a virtual conversation 
 with artist and author Robin Ha as we explore her New York Times 
 bestselling book\, Cook Korean!: A Comic Book Cookbook.\n\nFun to look at 
 and easy to use\, this unique combination of cookbook and graphic novel is 
 the ideal introduction to cooking Korean cuisine at home. Robin Ha’s 
 colorful and humorous one-to three-page comics fully illustrate the steps 
 and ingredients needed to bring more than sixty traditional (and some 
 not-so-traditional) dishes to life.\n\nIn these playful but exact recipes\, 
 you’ll learn how to create everything from easy kimchi (mak kimchi) and 
 soy garlic beef over rice (bulgogi dupbap) to seaweed rice rolls (gimbap) 
 and beyond. Friendly and inviting\, Cook Korean! is perfect for beginners 
 and seasoned cooks alike.\n\nEach chapter includes personal anecdotes and 
 cultural insights from Ha\, providing an intimate entry point for those 
 looking to try their hand at this cuisine.\n\nRegister today to discover 
 how easy\, approachable\, and fun authentic Korean cooking can be!\n\n 
 \n\nAbout the Author: \n\nRobin Ha is the New York Times bestselling author 
 and illustrator of Cook Korean!: A Comic Book with Recipes\, as well as the 
 graphic novel memoir Almost American Girl and historical fantasy graphic 
 novel The Fox Maidens. Her comics and illustrations have also appeared in 
 various publications\, including The Washington Post and The Los Angeles 
 Times\, as well as in anthologies highlighting Asian American culture: 
 Rise: A Pop History of Asian America from the Nineties to Now\, New 
 Frontiers: The Many Worlds of George Takei\, and Shattered: The Asian 
 American Comics Anthology (Secret Identities). Robin grew up reading and 
 drawing comics. At fourteen she moved to the United States from Seoul\, 
 Korea. She studied illustration at Rhode Island School of Design. She now 
 lives in Virginia.
LOCATION:Glen Ridge Free Public Library
ORGANIZER;CN="Glen Ridge Library":MAILTO:events@glenridgelibrary.org
CATEGORIES:Author Event/Talk
UID:LibCal-10286-17725369
URL:https://bccls.libcal.com/event/17725369
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Libcal Bccls 10315": "https://bccls.libcal.com/event/17761890"}
X-SOURCE-ID:libcal_bccls_10315
X-SOURCE:Libcal Bccls 10315
DTSTART:20261210T190000Z
DTEND:20261210T203000Z
DTSTAMP:20261210T000000Z
SUMMARY:Library Speakers Consortium - Comic Strips and Kimchi: Cooking Korean Food Made Easy with Robin Ha
DESCRIPTION:You must register here to attend:  
 https://libraryc.org/nutleypubliclibrary/225808\n\n\n\nCooking authentic 
 Korean food at home doesn't have to be intimidating or complicated—in 
 fact\, it can be as fun and engaging as reading your favorite graphic 
 novel! Join us for a virtual conversation with artist and author Robin Ha 
 as we explore her New York Times bestselling book\, Cook Korean!: A Comic 
 Book Cookbook.\n\nFun to look at and easy to use\, this unique combination 
 of cookbook and graphic novel is the ideal introduction to cooking Korean 
 cuisine at home. Robin Ha’s colorful and humorous one-to three-page 
 comics fully illustrate the steps and ingredients needed to bring more than 
 sixty traditional (and some not-so-traditional) dishes to life.\n\nIn these 
 playful but exact recipes\, you’ll learn how to create everything from 
 easy kimchi (mak kimchi) and soy garlic beef over rice (bulgogi dupbap) to 
 seaweed rice rolls (gimbap) and beyond. Friendly and inviting\, Cook 
 Korean! is perfect for beginners and seasoned cooks alike.\n\nEach chapter 
 includes personal anecdotes and cultural insights from Ha\, providing an 
 intimate entry point for those looking to try their hand at this 
 cuisine.\n\nRegister today to discover how easy\, approachable\, and fun 
 authentic Korean cooking can be!\n\nAbout the Author: \n\nRobin Ha is the 
 New York Times bestselling author and illustrator of Cook Korean!: A Comic 
 Book with Recipes\, as well as the graphic novel memoir Almost American 
 Girl and historical fantasy graphic novel The Fox Maidens. Her comics and 
 illustrations have also appeared in various publications\, including The 
 Washington Post and The Los Angeles Times\, as well as in anthologies 
 highlighting Asian American culture: Rise: A Pop History of Asian America 
 from the Nineties to Now\, New Frontiers: The Many Worlds of George Takei\, 
 and Shattered: The Asian American Comics Anthology (Secret Identities). 
 Robin grew up reading and drawing comics. At fourteen she moved to the 
 United States from Seoul\, Korea. She studied illustration at Rhode Island 
 School of Design. She now lives in Virginia.\n\n\n\n
LOCATION:Nutley Free Public Library
ORGANIZER;CN="Nutley Library":MAILTO:events@nutley.bccls.org
CATEGORIES:Author Event/Talk
UID:LibCal-10315-17761890
URL:https://bccls.libcal.com/event/17761890
END:VEVENT
